/**
\page org_mitk_views_ivim Intra-voxel incoherent motion estimation (IVIM)

This view fits the "intra-voxel incoherent motion" (IVIM) model and the diffusion kurtosis model to diffusion-weighted images and generates parameter maps. The input is a diffusion-weighted image acquired with several b-values; the gradient directions do not matter. IVIM needs several b-values below about 200 s/mm² to separate perfusion from diffusion, kurtosis needs b-values up to about 2000-3000 s/mm².

\section QmitkIVIMViewInput Input and preview

\li <b>Raw DWI</b>: the diffusion-weighted image.
\li <b>ROI</b> (optional): a binary 3D image or a segmentation; all labels of a segmentation together form the ROI, also labels hidden in the Segmentation view.

The plot shows the fit of the model selected by the tab (IVIM or Kurtosis) to the signal at the crosshair position; click around in the image to explore it. The fitted parameters are shown above the plot. With a ROI selected, the plot shows the fit to the mean signal of all image voxels whose centres lie inside the ROI instead, and the view reports how many voxels were averaged; it then no longer follows the crosshair. The preview fits the unsmoothed signal.

The buttons below the plot copy the b-values and measurements of the voxel (for IVIM as S/S0) with the fitted parameters ("Signal values to clipboard") or only the fitted parameters ("Fit-parameters to clipboard") to the clipboard, or save the plot as an image file.

<b>Generate Parameter Maps</b> fits every voxel of the image with the settings of the selected tab and adds the maps checked under "Output Maps" below the image in the Data Manager. With a ROI selected, only the voxels inside the ROI are fitted and the others get 0; the ROI must then have the size and geometry of the image, apart from rounding differences of up to 0.0001 mm in origin and spacing. Voxels that cannot be fitted, for example without signal or with too few usable measurements, get 0 too.

\section QmitkIVIMViewIvim IVIM

The IVIM model is a bi-exponential model of the signal ratio S/S0 with the perfusion fraction f, the diffusion coefficient D and the pseudo-diffusion coefficient D*: S/S0 = (1-f) exp(-bD) + f exp(-b(D+D*)). S0 is the mean of the b=0 volumes, the volumes whose b-value rounds to 0 s/mm². Without b=0 volumes the signal is divided by the mean of the volumes of the lowest b-value b<sub>min</sub>, which follows the same model at b - b<sub>min</sub> with a perfusion fraction f'; the fitted f' is converted to the fraction at b = 0 with the fitted D*: f/(1-f) = f'/(1-f') exp(b<sub>min</sub> D*). Without a D* (methods 3 and 4 when D* is not fitted) f is f', the fraction at b<sub>min</sub>. The fit methods differ in how they estimate the three parameters:

\li <b>1. Jointly fit D, f and D*</b>: all three parameters are estimated in one non-linear fit.
\li <b>2. Fit D &amp; f with fixed D* value</b>: only f and D are estimated; D* is the value of the D* slider.
\li <b>3. Fit D &amp; f (high b), then fit D*</b>: first f and D of the mono-exponential (1-f) exp(-bD) are fitted to the measurements with b-values above the "Ignore b&lt;" threshold, where the perfusion signal has decayed. Then D* is chosen with f and D fixed: the best of 100 values from 0.001 to 0.15 mm²/s in steps of 0.00149 mm²/s, refined between its two neighbours (D* stays at most 0.15 mm²/s).
\li <b>4. Linearly fit D &amp; f (high b), then fit D*</b>: as method 3, but f and D come from the straight line through the points (b, log S/S0) above the threshold.

The non-linear fits of methods 1 to 3 penalize D outside [0, 0.003] mm²/s and D* outside [0, 0.3] mm²/s; the line of method 4 is not bounded.

Parameters:
\li <b>D*</b> (method 2): the fixed D* in mm²/s.
\li <b>Signal threshold</b>: diffusion-weighted measurements at or below this value, in the units of the image, are left out of the fit. The "*" button sets it from the histogram of the b=0 volume: twice the most frequent value between the image minimum and half of the image maximum.
\li <b>Ignore b&lt; (first fit)</b> (methods 3 and 4): the b-value threshold in s/mm² of the first fit.
\li <b>Output Maps</b>: the maps of f, D and D* to generate, named "f Fit-n", "D Fit-n" and "D* Fit-n" with the method number n. For methods 3 and 4, D* is searched only when its map is checked; the preview always shows it.

The plot shows S/S0, the measurements of the first fit and, for methods 3 and 4, those of the D* search. Without b=0 volumes S0 is the b=0 signal of the fitted model.

\section QmitkIVIMViewKurtosis Kurtosis

The kurtosis model describes the signal with the apparent diffusion coefficient D and the kurtosis K: S = S0 exp(-bD + b²D²K/6). S0 is the mean of the b=0 volumes, as for IVIM the volumes whose b-value rounds to 0 s/mm². One D and one K are fitted to all diffusion-weighted volumes together, whatever their gradient direction. The straight fit therefore estimates the kurtosis of the direction-averaged signal (the mean signal kurtosis of dipy's MSDKI); the logarithmic fit gives the mean of the directional ADCs and, for evenly spread directions, the mean of the kurtosis tensor (MKT). Neither is the mean kurtosis (MK) of a kurtosis tensor fit, and in anisotropic tissue the two fit types give different K.

\li <b>Select Fit Type</b>: "Straight Fit" fits S, "Logarithmic Fit" fits log S; the logarithmic fit leaves out measurements at or below 0.
\li <b>Omit b=0 Measurements</b>: S0 is fitted as a third parameter instead of taken from the b=0 volumes. Without b=0 volumes, S0 is always fitted.
\li <b>Boundaries for K</b>: when on, K is kept within the selected range and D within [0, 0.004] mm²/s.
\li <b>Smoothing variance (mm²)</b>: the variance of a Gaussian smoothing applied to each volume before the maps are fitted; 0 means no smoothing.
\li <b>Output Maps</b>: the maps of D and K, named "Kurtosis_DMap_Method-..." and "Kurtosis_KMap_Method-..." with the fit type.

The plot shows S, the measurements and the fitted curve.

\section QmitkIVIMViewCmdApps Command line tools

MitkDiffusionIvimFit generates the IVIM maps (methods 1 to 4 as fit types 0 to 3, with the b threshold, the fixed D* and a mask as options, signal threshold 0). MitkDiffusionKurtosisFit generates the kurtosis maps with a straight fit, an optional mask, optional fitted S0 and K bounds (default -1000 to 1000), which also keep D within [0, 0.004] mm²/s; it always smooths with a variance of 1.5 mm².

